<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ===========================================================================
  THE HOMEPAGE'S OWN STYLESHEET
  ===========================================================================
  components/_homepage-sections.less is 3,000-odd lines that match nothing off
  this page - the banner, the category-tile reveal stage, the video showcase,
  the brand rail, the cascade finder and the split promo. It used to be part of
  css/spartrak-components.css, which is render-blocking on EVERY route, so
  every category page, product page, cart and checkout downloaded and parsed
  all of it to use none of it.

  MEASURED, gzipped, on the four bundles this split produced:

      cart / checkout / account   42,404 -> 17,328 B   -25,076
      product page                43,093 -> 23,365 B   -19,728
      category and search         42,404 -> 35,330 B    -7,074
      homepage                    42,404 -> 22,472 B   -19,932

  The homepage is not a regression despite gaining a second stylesheet: what it
  loses from the global bundle is larger than what this file adds back, because
  the global bundle was also carrying the PLP's and the PDP's components for it.

  WHY A SEPARATE REQUEST IS THE RIGHT TRADE HERE. Section 13 ranks fewer
  requests BELOW less CSS, and this is the case that ordering was written for:
  one extra HTTP/1.1 request against ~20KB of gzipped CSS that no page but this
  one can use. Both files are static, cacheable, and requested from the same
  origin in the same <head>, so they are fetched in parallel and neither
  delays the other's parse.

  THE HANDLE. `cms_index_index` is the home page's route handle, and it is
  already where Spartrak_Homepage mounts the sections themselves
  (app/code/Spartrak/Homepage/view/frontend/layout/cms_index_index.xml). So the
  markup and the CSS that styles it are declared on the same handle and cannot
  drift apart: a route that renders no home sections requests no home CSS.

  This file adds nothing else. The sections, their blocks and their view models
  stay in the module where they belong - a theme layout that re-declared them
  would be a second, weaker copy of that file.
-->
<page xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <head>
        <css src="css/spartrak-home.css"/>
    </head>
</page>
